1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,120
The saved reading index has eleven Week 11 rows. It lists all clinical
pathways, BCPEG treatment protocols, endocrine and immune review,
heart-failure types, clinical judgment and focused assessment,
diagnosis and planning, analyzing cues, pain and respiratory
pharmacology, mood disorders and diagnostic reasoning.

2
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:35,544
Nancy Caroline’s Canadian 8th edition is the learner-confirmed
principal examination text, but it does not replace any of the eleven
separate rows. Its selected chapter bridge is clearly labelled as
additional study.

3
00:00:35,544 --> 00:01:01,128
The eleven assigned readers now have source-specific English and
Chinese explanations, with the actual files and review extents
recorded. Repeated content retains genuine earlier source and meaning
checks. This is not merely a collection of candidate summaries. The
clinical rounds connect those readings to the patient; they do not
claim every linked image, video or assessment has been completed.

4
00:01:01,128 --> 00:01:32,040
The remaining differences are specific. The identified cardiovascular
book contains the named heart-failure chapter but not the school table
page range. The CPSC sedation table has duplicate score descriptions,
and the pharmacology table has an inhaler dose-unit error. The readers
explain the available assigned content and these corrections without
pretending the locators or products are interchangeable. Current law,
provider level and service guidance still govern actual care.

5
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:50,664
The BCEHS and BCPEG reading rows are not one interchangeable source.
BCPEG is a saved provincial examination snapshot. Current BCEHS pages
are service guidance. Read both when assigned, keep their revision
context visible and do not average conflicting instructions.

6
00:01:50,664 --> 00:02:06,984
For clinical use, check the current pathway and the line for your
provider level. The school snapshot can help with examination recall,
but a school page, a nursing textbook or a high-stakes exam diagram
does not independently authorize an intervention.

7
00:02:06,984 --> 00:02:21,840
Do not read only the sentence that contains a familiar term. Read the
page heading, surrounding paragraph, figure and provider-level notes.
Ask what the author is describing and which setting or profession the
statement belongs to.

8
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:36,096
Close the book and explain the mechanism in your own words. Then
connect it to one observation in the Week 11 cases. Keep a note of any
exact school edition, linked figure or lab detail that is still
missing.

9
00:02:36,096 --> 00:02:53,544
The two CNS page ranges teach different material. The first eleven
pages cover nutritional context, specialized diets and feeding routes;
the final five cover clinical judgment. Keeping the page range while
teaching only the model would not meet the school reading requirement.

10
00:02:53,544 --> 00:03:13,440
Connect the nutrition review to an incomplete medical history. Ask
about usual intake and recent change, an existing renal or
carbohydrate plan, medicine timing, swallowing difficulty or a feeding
device when relevant. These facts can change the questions and safety
plan without proving the cause.

11
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:28,992
The bilingual guide explains each diet and route category in the
actual assigned pages. It distinguishes US nutritional teaching from
individualized care and leaves the patient’s assessed feeding plan and
current service procedure intact.

12
00:03:28,992 --> 00:03:45,984
Enteral nutrition reaches the gastrointestinal tract through the
selected tube. Parenteral nutrition reaches the circulation through a
selected venous route. Neither label tells you that an unfamiliar tube
or line is correctly positioned or may be used by a PCP student.

13
00:03:45,984 --> 00:04:07,944
The assigned page confuses peripheral insertion with peripheral tip
location when discussing parenteral nutrition. A PICC is a central
line despite its insertion site. Selected NICE guidance also
recognizes suitable peripheral venous nutrition in specific short-term
circumstances, so not all parenteral nutrition is automatically
central.

14
00:04:07,944 --> 00:04:25,368
For this week, identify the existing device and plan, assess the
actual clinical concern and pass accurate information forward. Do not
turn textbook maintenance routines into permission to flush, restart a
feed or access a line whose use has not been authorized.

15
00:04:25,368 --> 00:04:41,592
The reading set links body mechanisms with bedside observation and
protocol use. A hypothesis should explain several cues, fit the timing
and survive comparison with alternatives. It remains a hypothesis
until the clinical picture supports it.

16
00:04:41,592 --> 00:05:00,840
For the bradycardia case, connect conduction, medication history,
renal context and perfusion. For the fall, connect anticoagulant use,
possible hidden bleeding, the limb exam and repeated distal checks.
Then use the current pathway and say what new finding would alter
urgency.

17
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:18,528
The actual school-folder copy now resolves the PMHN locator. Printed
pages four-eighty through five-oh-eight are physical pages four-
ninety-four through five-twenty-two. This is a bounded reading range,
not a claim to have covered every page of Chapter Sixteen.

18
00:05:18,528 --> 00:05:38,112
The middle of the assignment covers self-harm and suicide. A course
that teaches only depression and bipolar definitions would miss that
requirement. The bilingual reading explains assessment, immediate
safety and continuity without reproducing the textbook’s cases, answer
choices or hospital orders.

19
00:05:38,112 --> 00:05:53,064
Read the material as a US nursing text adapted to the school’s PCP
learning aim. Current BC clinical and legal sources remain separate; a
textbook page does not grant a medicine, restraint or disposition
authority.

20
00:05:53,064 --> 00:06:11,784
Low mood alone does not explain an entire presentation. Ask what
changed in interest, sleep, appetite, energy, thinking and daily
function, how long it lasted and how it differs from usual. A physical
complaint may be important even when a mood diagnosis is already
recorded.

21
00:06:11,784 --> 00:06:32,544
For an activated pattern, ask about increased activity and reduced
need for sleep over the relevant period. Mania is not simply being
cheerful or talking fast; a longitudinal professional assessment
determines the diagnosis. A known psychiatric history does not rule
out a new medical or toxicologic problem.

22
00:06:32,544 --> 00:06:45,192
Use the person’s own account, observed behaviour and attributed
collateral together. Record uncertainties and the immediate needs
rather than assigning a label to make an incomplete history appear
complete.

23
00:06:45,192 --> 00:07:06,288
The assigned pages introduce structured assessments, but the
favourable description of SAD PERSONS needs a visible correction.
Selected NICE guidance advises against using scales or global risk
categories to predict future suicide or decide treatment or discharge.
This is a current safety comparison, not BC law.

24
00:07:06,288 --> 00:07:25,080
Use calm direct questions about the present concern and listen without
judgment. Assess injury, illness and immediate safety alongside the
reported thoughts. A denial of a detailed plan is one finding, not
permission to stop assessing or leave without a defensible current
plan.

25
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:38,808
Communicate what is known, what remains unclear and who is responsible
during the next care transition. A useful record shows the patient’s
needs and actual actions; it does not replace them with a reassuring
score.

26
00:07:38,808 --> 00:07:56,928
The assigned lithium pages link medicine safety to kidney function,
hydration and interacting medicines. A patient with a mood diagnosis
and new vomiting, tremor, unsteadiness or confusion needs a medical
assessment; do not assume the change is only a mood episode.

27
00:07:56,928 --> 00:08:18,792
Do not use the book’s statement that toxicity begins at two-point-zero
as a reassuring cutoff. Current NHS Specialist Pharmacy Service
guidance notes that toxicity may occur despite an apparently normal
blood level. The field task is recognition, appropriate assessment and
timely clinical advice, not laboratory exclusion.

28
00:08:18,792 --> 00:08:33,600
Record the actual medicine history and the symptom timeline and follow
the local pathway for the patient’s condition. This course does not
instruct a student to prescribe, restart or independently adjust long-
term psychiatric medicines.

29
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:52,296
The actual CPSC range is more specific than the old generic focused-
assessment summary. It starts within neurological examination, crosses
breathing and circulation, and stops during abdominal examination.
Select the relevant findings without delaying the immediate
assessment.

30
00:08:52,296 --> 00:09:08,184
In our original parking-lot case, compare the new confusion with the
breathing pattern, perfusion and abdominal complaint. One source
heading does not make these four separate patients. The question is
how the findings change the current priority and next information
need.

31
00:09:08,184 --> 00:09:23,928
The paired reading gives the actual page-specific concepts and keeps
the hospital setting and source limitations visible. Use the existing
service assessment pathway for actions; do not add a nursing procedure
merely because it appears in the assigned text.

32
00:09:23,928 --> 00:09:40,824
The source’s sedation example repeats the same two and three
descriptions. We checked the rendered page, so this is not just a
text-extraction problem. Report the observed response and use the
exact approved tool; never repair a score by guessing its missing
anchor.

33
00:09:40,824 --> 00:09:59,520
Its pulse chart calls three normal, while other charts differ. Its
capillary-refill wording separates less than three from more than
three without defining exactly three. Describe what you observed and
identify the source rather than treating bare numbers as
interchangeable clinical conclusions.

34
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:12,552
A new decline in responsiveness or distal perfusion requires
reassessment in its own right. A score, an isolated saturation or a
completed checkbox does not cancel that clinical change.

35
00:10:12,552 --> 00:10:29,832
For the injured patient in the foyer, record the distal findings
before an authorized splint or movement and repeat them afterwards.
Say which side and site were checked and what was limited. Do not
write normal when sensation or movement could not be tested.

36
00:10:29,832 --> 00:10:46,152
For the breathless patient, report effort, speech, air entry and the
measured trend alongside the response to care. A sound label alone is
less useful than a clear account of the new abnormality, its
distribution and what changed.

37
00:10:46,152 --> 00:11:01,992
For abdominal or neurological findings, distinguish the patient’s
account from your observation and the possible explanation. Hand over
the remaining uncertainty rather than converting a limited field
examination into a definitive diagnosis.

38
00:11:01,992 --> 00:11:18,024
The assigned FON chapter teaches how nursing organizes responses,
risks and care plans. Understanding it supports clinical reasoning,
but it does not require a PCP to chart a nursing taxonomy or
independently adopt US nursing interventions.

39
00:11:18,024 --> 00:11:35,136
Tanner separates noticing, interpreting, responding and reflecting.
Reflection during care changes the immediate plan; reflection
afterwards guides a later attempt. The measurement model offers
another view of the reasoning, not a new treatment permission.

40
00:11:35,136 --> 00:11:54,120
Predict a plausible complication, prevent a modifiable risk, manage
the problem that is present and promote the person’s participation and
function. In the original two-patient scene, this means tracking both
the current breathing concern and the risk created by an unfinished
second-patient assessment.

41
00:11:54,120 --> 00:12:10,440
The book introduces actual versus potential and acute versus chronic
comparisons. They help organize questions but cannot replace the
clinical situation. A minor current complaint does not automatically
outrank a credible immediate threat to breathing.

42
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:26,496
Similarly, a familiar symptom in a chronic illness can worsen. Ask
about baseline, severity, new findings and the actual trend. Do not
use expected as a reason to ignore a change or use acute as a reason
to stop thinking.

43
00:12:26,496 --> 00:12:38,664
State the current priority and the evidence supporting it, then name
the finding that would make you revise the plan. This is the
connection between the assigned reading and the school’s semi-
autonomous scenario block.

44
00:12:38,664 --> 00:12:56,688
A useful goal describes an observable patient result, not just an
action completed. In the foyer case, assigning a partner to check the
second patient is a task. Receiving and using the report is evidence
that the communication loop happened; the patient’s response still
requires assessment.

45
00:12:56,688 --> 00:13:13,536
Make the goal realistic for the person’s baseline, available time and
resources. Identify who is acting and which dependency remains
unresolved. A requested crew or a message left for a service is not
automatically an accepted handover.

46
00:13:13,536 --> 00:13:25,656
Evaluate the actual result, record what remains incomplete and revise
the plan when it does not work. Do not declare success because a
checklist was signed or the original action was performed.

47
00:13:25,656 --> 00:13:39,432
Ejection fraction divides the amount ejected by the starting
ventricular volume. It is not the total amount ejected and is not the
cardiac output. The original diagram uses a common volume scale to
show the distinction.

48
00:13:39,432 --> 00:14:01,128
In Example A, one-hundred-twenty minus forty-eight gives seventy-two
millilitres, and seventy-two divided by one-hundred-twenty is sixty
percent. In Example B, eighty minus thirty-two gives forty-eight
millilitres, and forty-eight divided by eighty is also sixty percent.
The equal fractions describe unequal stroke volumes.

49
00:14:01,128 --> 00:14:19,128
Preserved fraction does not establish normal filling or exclude a
heart-failure syndrome. Conversely, neither of these hypothetical
numbers proves that a patient has heart failure. The school reading
locator and the actual book chapter are identified separately in the
paired guide.

50
00:14:19,128 --> 00:14:36,864
The chapter connects pressure, renal perfusion and myocardial stretch
to neural, hormonal and mechanical responses. Some initially support
flow, while natriuretic peptides oppose fluid retention. These are
mechanisms, not independent field treatment orders.

51
00:14:36,864 --> 00:14:53,712
Extra filling and increased vascular resistance can eventually
increase work and congestion in a failing heart. Volume and pressure
loading also favour different simplified remodeling patterns. More
wall thickness does not necessarily mean more effective function.

52
00:14:53,712 --> 00:15:09,768
Use this knowledge to explain the new breathlessness, position
intolerance or perfusion concern in the original case, without
assuming that every symptom comes from the old heart-failure
diagnosis. The current patient and authorized pathway determine the
action.

53
00:15:09,768 --> 00:15:38,304
The main chemical groups are amino-acid derivatives, peptides/proteins
and cholesterol-derived steroids. Catecholamines and thyroid hormones
are both amines, but have different solubility and receptor behavior.
Peptide hormones usually act at surface receptors; steroids and
thyroid hormones commonly use intracellular receptors. Transport-
protein binding and clearance affect the duration and measured free
versus total hormone.

54
00:15:38,304 --> 00:16:09,600
Response depends on hormone concentration, receptor number/sensitivity
and intracellular state. Upregulation and downregulation alter
responsiveness but are not absolute one-direction rules for every
disease. Permissive effects enable another signal, synergy amplifies
combined effects, and antagonism produces opposing effects, as with
many insulin/glucagon actions. Receptor resistance can cause disease
even when hormone is present.

55
00:16:09,600 --> 00:16:38,496
Anterior-pituitary GH promotes growth partly through IGF-1 and alters
fuel use; excess before versus after growth-plate closure produces
different patterns. Prolactin is under important inhibitory dopamine
control and rises with appropriate lactation stimulation, not only
during pregnancy. A single body feature or an isolated hormone value
is not sufficient to diagnose a growth or pituitary disorder in the
field.

56
00:16:38,496 --> 00:17:06,432
The hypothalamus links neural and endocrine control to the pituitary
through its stalk. Posterior pituitary is neural tissue that
stores/releases hypothalamic hormones; anterior pituitary is glandular
tissue receiving releasing/inhibiting signals through portal vessels.
Calling the pituitary the master gland does not mean it independently
controls every hormone or that the posterior lobe synthesizes ADH.

57
00:17:06,432 --> 00:17:33,864
Adrenal cortex has glomerulosa, fasciculata and reticularis zones,
associated mainly with mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids and adrenal
androgens respectively. The inner medulla contains chromaffin cells
and releases catecholamines after sympathetic stimulation. Cortex and
medulla share an organ but have different developmental origins,
signals and products; cortisol is not epinephrine.

58
00:17:33,864 --> 00:18:04,344
Insulin promotes GLUT4-mediated uptake in muscle/adipose tissue,
supports glycogen, fat and protein storage and reduces hepatic glucose
output. It does not open the glucose-entry gate in every cell: usual
brain and red-cell uptake is largely insulin independent. Insulin
deficiency also permits lipolysis and ketone production, so replacing
fluid or lowering a displayed glucose does not alone prove
ketoacidosis has resolved.

59
00:18:04,344 --> 00:18:29,448
The right lymphatic duct usually drains the right upper quadrant:
right head/neck, upper limb and thorax. The thoracic duct drains most
remaining regions, including both lower limbs, and returns at the left
venous angle. The right duct does not drain the entire right half of
the body. These routes explain anatomy without identifying an edema
cause from side alone.

60
00:18:29,448 --> 00:18:58,440
Lymph enters a node through afferent vessels, passes through sinuses
and leaves through efferent vessels. Nodes filter lymph and provide
sites for antigen presentation and lymphocyte activation.
Follicles/germinal centers are associated with B-cell responses; other
regions support T-cell interactions. A tender or enlarged node
suggests a process needing context, not automatic proof of one
infection or malignancy.

61
00:18:58,440 --> 00:19:29,304
Blood immune cells arise from hematopoietic precursors. Neutrophils
and macrophages are important phagocytes; monocytes circulate and can
contribute tissue populations. Dendritic cells are important antigen
presenters. B cells can form antibody-secreting plasma cells, T cells
coordinate or kill selected targets, and NK lymphocytes contribute
innate cytotoxicity. Not every lymphocyte is adaptive and not every T
cell secretes antibody.

62
00:19:29,304 --> 00:19:58,992
Innate recognition uses pattern-recognition systems for microbial
patterns and damaged-cell signals. Recognition can trigger
phagocytosis, mediator release and cellular recruitment; it is not
antigen-specific clonal memory in the usual B/T-cell sense.
Phagocytosis encloses material in a phagosome that can fuse with
lysosomal machinery. Some organisms evade killing, so engulfment does
not guarantee elimination.

63
00:19:58,992 --> 00:20:26,640
Cytokines coordinate cell behavior, and chemokines are a cytokine
group that guides migration. Interferons can promote antiviral states
in neighboring cells rather than directly acting as antibodies. Acute-
phase proteins such as CRP participate in responses but a raised CRP
does not identify the organism or diagnose sepsis alone. Chemotaxis
means directed movement along chemical signals.

64
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:54,672
Printed912 pairs10 to the eleventh power with “100trillion,” which is
arithmetically inconsistent; the comparison copy’s10 to the twelfth
still does not equal100trillion. Do not create a new exact repertoire
estimate from this typo. Learn combinatorial receptor diversity,
clonal selection and memory, and remember that antigen exposure does
not guarantee lifelong protection or symptom-free reinfection.

65
00:20:54,672 --> 00:21:28,776
Printed933 Table21.7 actually reverses the thyroid-antibody actions:
it labels Graves as receptor blockade and Hashimoto as receptor
stimulation. Do not memorize those cells. NIDDK explains Graves
through thyroid-stimulating antibodies and Hashimoto through immune-
mediated thyroid injury commonly causing reduced hormone output.
Hashimoto is not adequately defined as universal TSH-receptor
blockade, and a disease name alone does not establish this patient’s
current hormone state.

66
00:21:28,776 --> 00:21:59,592
Thyroid follicles take up iodide, incorporate iodine into
thyroglobulin-associated tyrosines and form T3/T4, storing precursor
in colloid before release. T4 has four iodines and T3 three; tissues
convert some T4 to the more active T3. TRH/TSH and thyroid-hormone
feedback regulate the axis. Transport-protein-bound hormone and free
hormone are not interchangeable measurements.

67
00:21:59,592 --> 00:22:41,640
Chapter17 Figure17.19’s70-110mg/dL band is an illustration in this
school copy, not the current BCEHS hypoglycemia intervention gate or a
universal diagnostic reference range. Do not average it with the
comparison copy’s70-100. Explain insulin/glucagon feedback, then
interpret a measured glucose with units, symptoms, timing and the
current pathway. The accompanying statement that brain/liver/kidney
cells lack insulin receptors is not a sound general rule; insulin-
independent uptake is not the same as absence of insulin signaling.

68
00:22:41,640 --> 00:23:15,600
The assigned endocrine glossary endpoint includes acromegaly,
cortex/medulla, ACTH, aldosterone, alpha/beta/delta cells, ANP,
calcitonin, cAMP/DAG, EPO, FSH and G protein. Retrieve each as source,
stimulus and effect, rather than equating a definition with treatment.
Cortisol supports hepatic glucose availability through several
metabolic effects; it should not be reduced to the glossary’s
glycogen-catabolism wording.

69
00:23:15,600 --> 00:23:47,928
Week11 original integration: a person with diabetes and a transplant
becomes confused and breathless but has no fever. Link glucose fuel
balance, fluid loss, immunosuppression and infection risk without
assuming one diagnosis. Obtain actual glucose and repeated
respiratory/perfusion findings, clarify medicines and recent changes,
and act on present threats. Neither an absent fever nor correction of
one measured value proves the remaining illness has resolved.

70
00:23:47,928 --> 00:24:15,264
Nociceptive pain comes from tissue-related noxious stimulation:
somatic pain involves structures such as skin, muscle and bone, while
visceral pain arises from internal organs and may be diffuse or
referred. Neuropathic pain involves a lesion or disease of the
somatosensory system and may be burning, shooting or tingling.
Descriptors suggest mechanisms but do not establish a diagnosis alone.

71
00:24:15,264 --> 00:24:43,488
The five-step model is transduction, conduction, transmission,
modulation and perception. A noxious stimulus activates a nociceptor;
electrical activity travels through peripheral pathways and relays
toward the brain. Spinal and descending systems can amplify or inhibit
signaling, and conscious experience integrates those signals with
context. A protective withdrawal reflex need not wait for conscious
perception.

72
00:24:43,488 --> 00:25:08,496
Pain threshold is the point at which a stimulus becomes painful, not
the maximum pain a person can tolerate. Expectations, prior
experience, support and physiology affect the experience and
expression of pain. Use an appropriate self-report or observational
tool, record function and reassess with a comparable method; a score
alone is not a judgment of credibility.

73
00:25:08,496 --> 00:25:34,080
Acetaminophen is analgesic and antipyretic, with little clinically
useful peripheral anti-inflammatory action. Its analgesic mechanism is
not completely settled. Ask about every combination cold, sleep and
prescription product, total amount and timing, liver disease and
alcohol exposure. An apparently ordinary product can contribute to
serious cumulative toxicity.

74
00:25:34,080 --> 00:26:01,680
Aspirin, ibuprofen and naproxen are NSAIDs. COX inhibition explains
analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory effects, but also GI
injury/bleeding and impaired renal perfusion in susceptible people.
Dehydration, kidney disease, ulcer/bleeding history, anticoagulants
and other NSAIDs change risk; taking a drug with food does not
eliminate ulcer or bleeding risk.

75
00:26:01,680 --> 00:26:29,640
Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX, so its antiplatelet effect
lasts beyond its presence in plasma. An analgesic choice is not
interchangeable with the separate suspected-ACS aspirin pathway. Ask
about bleeding, aspirin-sensitive asthma, hypersensitivity and other
medicines; pediatric viral-illness/Reye syndrome warnings also prevent
casually transferring adult pain advice to children.

76
00:26:29,640 --> 00:26:59,760
The book places tramadol under nonopioid analgesics, although it has
opioid-receptor activity. Teach it as an opioid analgesic with
additional norepinephrine/serotonin effects, not as a harmless
nonopioid or a guarantee of partial-agonist safety. Sedative
combinations, respiratory depression, seizures and serotonergic
interactions matter. Neuropathic-pain adjuncts such as duloxetine,
pregabalin and local anesthetics have different targets and risks.

77
00:26:59,760 --> 00:27:31,368
Opioid agonists activate receptors involved in pain modulation,
including mu receptors; analgesia and sedation/respiratory depression
can occur through related receptor actions. Morphine is a classic
reference agent. Fentanyl is synthetic, hydromorphone and oxycodone
are semisynthetic, and heroin is semisynthetic rather than a naturally
occurring poppy constituent. Similar class membership does not make
dose, potency, route or duration interchangeable.

78
00:27:31,368 --> 00:28:01,608
Codeine depends substantially on CYP2D6 conversion to morphine;
genetic variation and interacting drugs can make effect less
predictable. Meperidine has a neurotoxic metabolite of concern,
especially with impaired clearance. Methadone can have prolonged and
variable effects. Fentanyl patches and extended-release oral opioids
are not rapid rescue substitutes; do not crush modified-release
formulations or improvise conversions.

79
00:28:01,608 --> 00:28:30,912
Nausea, constipation, urinary retention, sedation and hypotension can
accompany opioid treatment. Slow or shallow breathing and reduced
responsiveness demand immediate airway/breathing assessment, not just
another pain score. Alcohol, benzodiazepines and other sedatives
increase risk. A previous high tolerated dose does not establish
safety after an interruption or in a new acute illness.

80
00:28:30,912 --> 00:28:56,496
Clarify an allergy history: itching/flushing associated with histamine
release is not automatically the same as anaphylaxis, but reported
airway swelling or a severe reaction cannot be dismissed. The book’s
different-structure fentanyl statement is not a guarantee of allergy
safety. Document the actual reaction and follow the authorized drug
pathway and consultation requirements.

81
00:28:56,496 --> 00:29:26,016
Tolerance is reduced effect after repeated exposure; physical
dependence means adaptation that can produce withdrawal when exposure
falls. Opioid use disorder involves a problematic pattern with
impairment or distress, not merely taking a prescribed opioid or
having tolerance/withdrawal under appropriate medical supervision. Use
nonjudgmental questions about amount, source, timing and co-use rather
than diagnosing addiction from a pain request.

82
00:29:26,016 --> 00:30:01,320
Naloxone competitively antagonizes opioid effects. For a patient with
inadequate breathing, support airway and ventilation while following
the current opioid-poisoning pathway; the clinical endpoint is
improved spontaneous ventilation, not forcing complete wakefulness.
Recurrent depression is possible because the opioid can outlast
reversal. The book’s20-30minute statement and BCEHS
monograph’s30-45minute IM/IV description are not a universal timer or
discharge rule.

83
00:30:01,320 --> 00:30:30,912
Naloxone can precipitate withdrawal and other adverse responses; the
source phrase “only major adverse effect” is too absolute. Naltrexone
is a longer-term antagonist treatment, not the same emergency reversal
product. Nalbuphine is a mixed agonist/antagonist analgesic, not an
interchangeable rescue antidote. Know the distinctions without
importing the book’s hospital schedules or creating a new PCP drug
entitlement.

84
00:30:30,912 --> 00:31:01,368
Original retrieval: a patient with a painful wrist has taken a
combination cold remedy and an unknown pain tablet, then becomes
difficult to keep awake. First assess breathing, responsiveness and
perfusion; obtain products, amounts, times and co-ingestants. Less
reported pain with falling alertness is not successful analgesia.
Reassess both comfort/function and safety, and explain the medication
uncertainty in handover.

85
00:31:01,368 --> 00:31:30,624
Obstruction limits airflow through narrowed airways; restriction
limits expansion or available lung volume. Asthma includes variable
narrowing and inflammation. COPD includes chronic airway and alveolar
abnormalities; emphysema involves destruction of alveolar walls and
loss of elastic recoil, not just the book’s generic airway-damage
description. A bronchodilator does not reverse every cause of
breathlessness.

86
00:31:30,624 --> 00:31:56,568
Beta2 agonists relax bronchial smooth muscle. Albuterol is the US name
for salbutamol; levalbuterol is another short-acting agent. Salmeterol
is long acting and is not a substitute for immediate rescue in an
acute attack. Do not generalize that all beta stimulation relaxes all
tissues: cardiac beta effects differ, and beta2 selectivity is not
absolute.

87
00:31:56,568 --> 00:32:23,688
Check tremor, palpitations, heart rate/rhythm, breathing and the
actual response after a beta agonist. A beta blocker or other
sympathomimetic can change the response. Reduced wheeze alone is not
enough: air entry, speech, effort, fatigue, mentation and oxygenation
can reveal continuing or worsening obstruction, including a very quiet
chest with poor airflow.

88
00:32:23,688 --> 00:32:59,928
A source-table safety discrepancy is visible: printed691/692 describes
albuterol inhalations as2.5mg per inhalation. Do not use that
statement to dose an MDI or convert a nebulizer solution into puffs.
The current BCEHS monograph lists a100mcg/spray MDI separately
from2.5mg/mL nebules. Verify product, concentration, route, device and
the current age/weight/provider-specific instruction before
calculating.

89
00:32:59,928 --> 00:33:29,040
An inhaler is a drug-device combination. A metered-dose aerosol, dry-
powder device and nebulizer require different preparation and
inhalation technique. Do not apply the book’s generic four-spray
priming, fifteen-second interval or water-cleaning advice to every
device. Check the actual instructions, use the appropriate spacer when
indicated, assess technique and use teach-back; powder capsules
intended for inhalation are not swallowed.

90
00:33:29,040 --> 00:33:57,096
Ephedrine and epinephrine have broader adrenergic effects than a
selective inhaled beta2 agonist. Epinephrine’s alpha effects support
vasoconstriction and its beta effects include cardiac stimulation and
bronchodilation. The textbook’s oral/inhaled/IV/SC/IM list is not
permission to interchange routes or use a consumer inhaler instead of
the authorized anaphylaxis pathway.

91
00:33:57,096 --> 00:34:29,832
Ipratropium blocks muscarinic acetylcholine effects on bronchial
smooth muscle; tiotropium provides longer maintenance bronchodilation.
For the current BCEHS severe-bronchospasm pathway, ipratropium is an
adjunct with salbutamol, not acute monotherapy; its repeat
instructions are not copied from the textbook. Dry mouth, blurred
vision, urinary-retention risk and paradoxical worsening merit
attention. Avoid aerosol exposure to the eyes, especially with narrow-
angle glaucoma.

92
00:34:29,832 --> 00:35:01,920
Corticosteroids act through glucocorticoid receptors and altered
inflammatory signaling, including gene regulation; “stabilizing
lysosomes” is not a complete mechanism. Beclomethasone, fluticasone
and budesonide are inhaled examples, while prednisone and
methylprednisolone illustrate systemic treatment. Anti-inflammatory
benefit is not instantaneous smooth-muscle relaxation, and an inhaled
steroid alone is not the rescue bronchodilator for acute severe
obstruction.

93
00:35:01,920 --> 00:35:36,408
Inhaled steroids can cause local candidiasis and dysphonia;
appropriate mouth rinsing and device technique reduce local problems.
Systemic exposure can affect glucose, mood, infection risk, blood
pressure and other functions. HPA suppression and stopping/tapering
decisions depend on exposure and clinical context: do not tell every
short-course patient they must taper or that inhaled and systemic
risks are identical. Suspected adrenal illness needs assessment, not a
self-designed taper.

94
00:35:36,408 --> 00:36:12,216
Theophylline and aminophylline are xanthines with bronchodilator
effects and a narrow therapeutic margin. The source’s10-20mcg/mL range
is a textbook reference, not a PCP titration target. Vomiting,
agitation, tremor, confusion, tachyarrhythmia or seizures can indicate
toxicity; interactions and altered clearance matter. Obtain the
formulation, last dose, recent illness and medication changes, and do
not treat this as an invitation to initiate an infusion.

95
00:36:12,216 --> 00:36:41,136
Leukotrienes contribute to bronchoconstriction, mucus and
inflammation. Montelukast and zafirlukast block leukotriene receptors;
zileuton inhibits leukotriene synthesis. These are not one identical
mechanism or acute rescue substitutes. Oral montelukast is not an
inhaled dry powder. Hepatic risk and interactions differ between
products, so a shared class label does not replace a product-specific
history.

96
00:36:41,136 --> 00:37:08,016
Cromolyn stabilizes mast cells and reduces mediator release; its role
is prevention rather than immediate bronchodilation. It is not
naloxone, an antihistamine or a rescue replacement for epinephrine in
anaphylaxis. Cough or worsening wheeze after an inhaled product still
requires reassessment. Histamine is one mediator, not the entire
explanation of every allergic response.

97
00:37:08,016 --> 00:37:38,880
Montelukast has a serious neuropsychiatric warning, including
behavior/mood changes and suicidal thoughts or actions, with or
without prior psychiatric illness. The source’s blanket “do not stop”
teaching must not override the regulator warning to stop montelukast
and contact a clinician immediately for new neuropsychiatric symptoms.
Assess immediate safety and report the timing; do not assume a new
mental-health presentation is unrelated to medicines.

98
00:37:38,880 --> 00:38:11,976
Original retrieval: a breathless patient points to a daily tiotropium
device and says, “I already used my rescue.” Identify the actual
product and rescue plan, assess speech/effort/air entry/mentation and
look for deterioration. Explain why maintenance bronchodilation and
anti-inflammatory control differ from acute rescue. After authorized
care, compare the whole respiratory picture with baseline rather than
counting puffs as proof of improvement.

99
00:38:11,976 --> 00:38:41,256
Page38 starts IV therapy with a reason: access for an indicated
medicine or appropriate fluid replacement. Then maintain asepsis while
inspecting the catheter, solution and set, priming tubing,
selecting/preparing a site, establishing access, connecting,
controlling flow and securing. An open vein does not itself prove a
fluid indication, and successful insertion does not finish patient or
site monitoring.

100
00:38:41,256 --> 00:39:15,816
Page39’s correct dimensional relationship is volume in millilitres
times the tubing drop factor in drops per millilitre, divided by
minutes. Its worked examples incorrectly label the factor as drops per
minute, and one solution description combines5% with D10W; do not copy
either error into a label or calculation. Original arithmetic:90mL
over45minutes through a10drops/mL set gives20drops/minute. This is
arithmetic, not a fluid prescription.

101
00:39:15,816 --> 00:39:45,864
The same page describes an approximate75mL/hour maintenance reference
and repeated checks of ABCs, observations, injury sites, IV site,
tubing, bag, rate and infused volume. That reference is not a
universal adult, child, heart-failure or renal-failure rate. Confirm
the actual ordered plan and repeat assessment sooner if the patient,
site or flow changes rather than waiting for a scheduled interval.

102
00:39:45,864 --> 00:40:12,912
The page40 adult diagram links arrest recognition to CPR and early AED
analysis. With basic bag-mask care it uses30compressions to2breaths; a
shock-advised branch delivers the shock then resumes CPR, and the no-
shock branch also resumes CPR before the next analysis cycle. No shock
advised does not mean circulation is present. Organized electrical
activity alone does not prove a pulse.

103
00:40:12,912 --> 00:40:51,576
The school2025 page40 lists30minutes in its continuation box;
pages42-45 also describe medical-oversight consultation and
reversible-cause/transport decisions, including a20minute consultation
point. The later public copy adds a30/40minute distinction and changes
several consultation statements. These are materially different texts,
not one blended timer. Current N02 directs resuscitation decision-
making to its relevant pathway and clinical consultation; an elapsed
time alone is not termination authority.

104
00:40:51,576 --> 00:41:27,384
Pages42-43 emphasize rate100-120/minute, full recoil, minimal
interruption, early defibrillation and compressor rotation when
possible. The adult reference is5-6cm; the school also prints a broad
one-third-to-one-half size adjustment that must not become a universal
adult depth rule. The selected current pediatric comparison uses at
least one-third chest depth, approximately4cm for infants and5cm for
children, rather than deliberately targeting one-half.

105
00:41:27,384 --> 00:42:04,152
The page41 infant/child diagram uses30:2 for one rescuer and15:2 for
two; it also places neonatal3:1 in the same diagram. Page44 separately
says no AED for a neonate. Do not follow the shared graphic’s shock
loop as a neonatal AED instruction or apply birth-transition3:1 to
every later infant collapse. First identify the age, circumstances and
correct resuscitation framework; pediatric BLS and resuscitation at
birth are not the same algorithm.

106
00:42:04,152 --> 00:42:42,264
Page44’s2025 newborn sequence starts with an under100 description but
uses under60 to move through its early ventilation steps; the2026
comparison changes those early gates to under100, including a later
transition. Neither old staircase should replace the current neonatal
algorithm. In the current framework, apnea/gasping or heart rate
below100 requires positive-pressure ventilation; compressions are
considered for persistent rate below60 after effective ventilation and
correction, not simply for every rate below100.

107
00:42:42,264 --> 00:43:18,096
Page46 distinguishes an effective cough from severe obstruction and
then loss of responsiveness. The selected AHA2025 comparison uses five
back blows then five abdominal thrusts for responsive adults/children
with severe obstruction; infants receive back blows and chest thrusts,
not abdominal thrusts. If unresponsive, start CPR with compressions
and remove only a visible object when opening the airway. Do not use
the school’s initial failed-breath wording to delay that response, and
never perform blind finger sweeps.

108
00:43:18,096 --> 00:43:55,560
Page50’s fixed-flow/COPD and blanket high-flow statements differ from
current assessment-led oxygen guidance. Current A07 gives a general94%
target and a chronic-hypoxemia intervention band of88-90%, while the
complaint-specific B05 text uses88-92%. Keep the named source and
patient context visible rather than average these bands. Critical
illness and unreliable oximetry require active support; do not
withhold needed oxygen to obtain an untreated number.

109
00:43:55,560 --> 00:44:29,664
That page’s statement that every adult mask below6L/min delivers less
oxygen than room air is not a reliable universal device rule. Low flow
in a simple mask can allow exhaled gas rebreathing, and
reservoir/device performance matters. Flow in litres/minute, delivered
oxygen fraction and airway pressure are different quantities. Choose
the actual device instructions and current pathway; more flow does not
correct an obstructed airway or absent ventilation by itself.

110
00:44:29,664 --> 00:44:59,448
Pages51-52 describe CPAP as continuous pressure during spontaneous
breathing, supporting recruitment and reducing work of breathing in
selected patients. It does not deliver assisted breaths for a patient
whose effort is failing. Positive intrathoracic pressure can also
reduce venous return and blood pressure. Reassess breathing,
alertness, mask tolerance, oxygenation and perfusion, not only the
saturation display.

111
00:44:59,448 --> 00:45:41,400
The school CPAP page requires age13orabove, wakefulness/commands, an
open airway and its listed distress criteria, including rate above24,
saturation below94% on oxygen and accessory-muscle use. It excludes
impaired airway protection, hypoventilation/arrest, aspiration risk,
poor mask fit, traumatic respiratory distress, tracheostomy, suspected
pneumothorax and systolic pressure below90. Current PR09 also
specifies the relevant CPAP training and recommends PCP consultation.
Reading the table does not replace either requirement.

112
00:45:41,400 --> 00:46:10,320
Page52 limits CPAP and PEEP escalation to10cmH2O, while page53’s
device chart also displays higher columns. A displayed column is not
permission to exceed the procedure cap. Nor does a litre/minute flow
number equal a centimetre-of-water pressure. Current PR09 follows the
manufacturer flow chart and its own escalation limit; no unverified
chart for a different device is substituted.

113
00:46:10,320 --> 00:46:47,664
The school page55 lists prior allergy history among its criteria; the
later public copy removes it. Do not wait for a known prior allergy,
rash or profound hypotension before recognizing a compatible systemic
reaction. Current E09 prioritizes intramuscular epinephrine and ABC
support; salbutamol is an adjunct after epinephrine, and
antihistamines do not replace it. The school lists diphenhydramine
under PCP, whereas current E09 places it under ACP; the exam page does
not expand current authority.

114
00:46:47,664 --> 00:47:26,760
Page56 emphasizes reduced responsiveness with slow, limited-volume
breathing. Support airway and effective ventilation, obtain exposure
history and glucose, and use the current opioid pathway rather than
waiting for naloxone to work. Its glucose cross-reference uses less-
than-or-equal4mmol/L, whereas page57 and current E01 define the
correction gate as below4. Keep the exact inequality difference
visible; measurement quality, symptoms and the active pathway
determine the response, not an invented compromise cutoff.

115
00:47:26,760 --> 00:48:02,904
BVM with PEEP adds end-expiratory pressure to assisted breaths and
depends on an effective closed mask seal. It is not CPAP delivered to
an apneic patient. Current general PR10 excludes adult systolic
pressure at or below90, cardiac arrest, suspected pneumothorax and
traumatic respiratory arrest, with age-specific pediatric pressure
limits; neonatal specialty instructions are a separate context. Do not
turn the school’s8-10/minute PEEP paragraph into a rate for every
child, newborn or resuscitation.

116
00:48:02,904 --> 00:48:45,240
Page57 includes unexplained altered consciousness even without known
diabetes. Check airway/breathing, glucose and stroke features, then
select a permitted route using airway protection and access. Its exam-
copy PCP box lists D10W10-25g as100-250mL of10% solution and glucagon
when IV access is unavailable; verify the current monograph rather
than treating that box as an independent prescription. An oral product
is not safe merely because it can enter the mouth. Recheck glucose and
mentation and investigate persistent illness.

117
00:48:45,240 --> 00:49:13,872
Original retrieval: a dyspneic patient initially follows commands on
CPAP but becomes drowsy with weaker, shallower breaths and worsening
perfusion. State the changed findings, recognize that the original
CPAP assumptions no longer hold, and move to the appropriate
supported-airway/ventilation plan with help. Turning up pressure to
reach a saturation number does not fix lost respiratory effort or
airway protection.

118
00:49:13,872 --> 00:49:25,992
Use this retrieval sequence after each guide. The purpose is not to
memorize a diagnosis label; it is to show how the reading informs one
defensible decision and what you would reassess.

119
00:49:25,992 --> 00:49:40,008
A strong answer identifies a finding, explains why it matters, names
uncertainty and stays at the right provider level. If the source
edition or page is not verified, say so instead of guessing.
